            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"The archetype of the natural, striving human (the supplanter) who is transformed by God's grace into a spiritual overcomer (Israel), as well as a corporate symbol for God's chosen, covenant people elected by grace.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"Genesis 25:26\",\n    \"Genesis 32:24-28\",\n    \"Romans 9:11-13\",\n    \"Hosea 12:3-4\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"The fleshly, carnal, or weak nature of a believer, frequently contrasted by prophets and expositors with 'Israel', which represents the mature, spiritually victorious nature.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Isaiah 41:14\",\n        \"Amos 7:2\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"A corporate symbol for the New Testament Church as the spiritual Israel.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Galatians 6:16\",\n        \"Romans 9:6\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"A typological prefiguring of Christ, particularly in his exile to a far country to serve for and secure a bride (representing the Church).\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Genesis 29:15-20\",\n        \"Ephesians 5:25\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"minority\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": true,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"In Christian exposition, Jacob is frequently analyzed through the etymology of his names. 'Jacob' (heel-catcher/supplanter) is universally used to illustrate human scheming, self-reliance, and the flesh, while his renamed identity 'Israel' represents divine transformation, prevailing faith, and spiritual blessing. Romans 9 also universally cements Jacob as the primary biblical symbol of God's sovereign election by grace independent of human merit.\"\n}",
